extraction_notes: "Page span: the heading and lore start at the foot of printed 180, the stat block fills printed 181 beside a full-height illustration, and equipment, money and cybernetics end on printed 182, where the ISS Specter begins. GROUP: the book files the three ISS classes in their own Coalition Police & Law Enforcement section (printed 178; the contents list them as CS ISS O.C.C.s beside the CS O.C.C.s). None of the five groups names police. It is filed men-of-arms with men_of_arms true because the Peacekeeper is the uniformed, armored beat cop whose skills the book says centre on combat and riot control, it pilots SAMAS power armor, it serves under a military command structure, and the book''s experience tables (printed 224) give it the same ladder as the RPA Elite SAMAS pilot. MONEY: 3D4x1000 credits to start, printed outright, so that is stored rather than a month''s pay; the monthly salary of 4,200 credits and the free apartment are in restrictions. The text layer reads the dice as a cipher; 3D4x1000 and 4200 confirmed from a render. BONUSES: the book prints initiative at levels 1, 3, 7, 10 and 14, and one level list for both saves; the +1 vs magic illusion and mind control and the +2 vs horror factor are both read as falling at levels 2, 4, 7, 10, 12 and 15, each step cumulative. INVESTIGATION is a special skill printed with its own base (25% +5% per level) and is kept as a special ability; the catalog''s Crime Scene Investigation is a different skill. LITERACY is printed bare and stored as Literacy: Native Language (American), as the RCSG Scientist does. The book prints Pilot: Robots & Power Armor: Basic, stored as the Robots & Power Armor row with no bonus. Hand to Hand: Expert changes to Martial Arts, or Assassin if evil, for one related skill. The book prints no language line, so none is stored. ARMOR: old style Dead Boy armor, which printed 104 names as the CA-1 heavy or CA-2 light; offered as a choice. The book prints energy rifle and energy sidearm of choice; the lists offer the same CS rows the military classes of this book use. The C-5 pump pistol, which the weapons chapter says ISS urban forces use widely, fires explosive cartridges and is not an energy sidearm, so it is not offered. Four E-clips for each weapon are stored as eight. The neuro-mace is the Neural Mace row; the silver plated survival knife is the Knife, Silver Plated row; the nightstick is the Fiberglass Nightstick row (the only nightstick rows are Heroes Unlimited''s); four tear gas grenades are the Triax tear gas grenade row and two flash grenades the stun/flash grenade row; the flares are signal flares; the silver cross is the small silver cross; six wooden stakes are the six-stake row; handcuffs are the regular handcuffs row. Vehicles are prose, as the Side Kick RPA does; see BOOK-INGEST-AUDIT.md F3."
---

## Lore

Peacekeepers are the uniformed street police of the Coalition''s cities: beat
cops in old style Dead Boy armor or SAMAS power armor who break up fights,
direct traffic, watch for criminals and monsters and answer emergencies on sky
cycles. Their visibility is the point - a daily show of strength that keeps the
peace and reassures citizens. Crimes that need long investigation go to the
Inspectors, much as pre-Rifts detectives took over from uniformed officers.

## GM Notes

The ISS is police, not military: the Army enters a city only when invited by the
Emperor, the regional ISS head, the Governor or the High-Mayor, and the latter
three only in an emergency. ISS officers may search, wiretap, enter homes
secretly and hold suspects for 72 hours on suspicion alone. They treat ordinary
citizens with restraint and are widely seen as heroes, but practitioners of
magic, unregistered psychics, cyborgs, Crazies and Juicers are always treated as
armed and dangerous, and nonhumans and the supernatural are met with lethal
force. The ISS polices its own harshly; traitors in the ranks may simply
disappear. Armor often carries CPD or ISS lettering.
